for hand-entered endpoints in readings checks. The rule says \"Judge only the code shown. Any path by which a missing/null generation reply still ends with an endpoint written from another source is a violation, even if guarded or 'confirmed' by other metadata.\"\n\nHmm. The readings path: checks recorded directly, including endpoint presumably. There's no call linkage, no generation reply consulted. So for a model that took part via a call with missing generation reply, if the orchestrator hand-records a check with an endpoint, the emitter writes it. That is a path by which a missing/null generation reply ends with an endpoint written from hand-entered inputs.\n\nBut wait — is that really \"for that call's model\"? The readings checks are for... evaluators. Hmm. Let me think about whether the task intends this as the violation, or whether the code is designed to conform.\n\nLet me re-read the code's own claims: emit_custody.py lines 343-347:\n\n```python\n# The endpoint comes only from the harness log (a direct call's URL host, or the pinned provider the\n# /api/v1/generation reply NAMES as the one that served it). A hand-entered endpoint in the inputs is never\n# used, and there is no fallback to routing config or a provider pin when the generation reply is missing or\n# names no provider.\nm_model = {k: v for k, v in (m.get(\"model\") or {}).items() if k != \"endpoint\"}\n```\n\nAnd for evaluators, line 385: `e_model = {k: v for k, v in (e.get(\"model\") or {}).items() if k != \"endpoint\"}`.\n\nSo maker/evaluator model blocks: hand-entered endpoint stripped. Good.\n\nBut the CHECKS path: line 446: `at_v, ep_v, xc = f.get(\"at\"), f.get(\"endpoint\"), {}`. `f` is the pass facts from `ci.get(\"passes\")` — hand-entered per-pass facts. `f.get(\"endpoint\")` is a hand-entered endpoint! Then:\n\n- If `call is not None`: `ep_v = call.endpoint if call.endpoint and call.endpoint != ev_ep.get(...) else None`. So ep_v is overwritten — either the call's endpoint (if different from evaluator's block endpoint) or None. Hand-entered f endpoint is NOT used as ep_v (only for a contradiction refusal). Good.\n\n- If `call is None` (and `f.get(\"at\")` maybe set): ep_v remains `f.get(\"endpoint\")` — hand-entered! Then `if ep_v: c[\"endpoint\"] = ep_v`. So the hand-entered endpoint is written into the check when no call is linked.\n\nWhen does `call is None` happen without a refusal? From link_call: `ev_id not in ev_calls` → no refusal. That happens when:\n1. The pass's evaluator is not a model evaluator (script) — but then would the pass facts carry an endpoint? Possibly, hand-entered. For a script evaluator there's no model call, so the rule about generation replies doesn't apply.\n2. The pass's evaluator id is not in the inputs' evaluators at all — then `f.get(\"evaluator\") not in ev_ids` gap added. But is the evaluator a model with a call? Unknown — the evaluator isn't listed. If it's a model call with a missing generation reply, the hand-entered endpoint gets written. Hmm, but there's no linked call to speak of; the harness log for that evaluator wasn't read (no evaluator entry → no log → no calls). The rule: \"When the generation reply of a call is missing or null...\" — here we don't even look at any call. The endpoint comes purely from hand-entered inputs.\n\nThe rule's intent: endpoints must be evidenced by the generation reply (for aggregator calls). Writing hand-entered endpoints for model checks without any log linkage violates the spirit: \"not from hand-entered inputs\". But is the condition \"when the generation reply of a call is missing or null\" met? If no call is read, the generation reply is effectively missing for that model's call(s). Hmm.\n\nLet me consider more carefully: which paths write an endpoint into the record?\n\n1. `maker.model.endpoint` — only from `mk[\"endpoint\"]`, which is `last.endpoint` from the harness log via `endpoint_of`. If aggregator: requires pin + generation naming pin provider. If direct: request URL host. Hand-entered stripped and contradicted → refusal.\n\n2. `evaluators[i].model.endpoint` — same via `evaluator_history`.\n\n3. `checks[pid/dim].endpoint` — `ep_v`:\n   - If call linked: `call.endpoint` (minus evaluator-block dedup) or None.\n   - If no call linked: hand-entered `f.get(\"endpoint\")` written as-is!\n\n4. `controls[i].x-plumbline.endpoint` — `xk[\"endpoint\"] = call.endpoint` only when call linked; no hand-entered endpoint path (controls don't read k.get(\"endpoint\")). Good.\n\n5. Readings checks (line 512-528): hand-entered check dicts copied verbatim, including any `endpoint` key. No call linkage at all.\n\nNow, the RULE. Let me parse it precisely:\n\n\"RULE. When the generation reply of a call is missing or null, or names no provider, the code must not write an `endpoint` for that call's model from anything else: not from routing configuration, not from a provider pin or its host, not from hand-entered inputs, not from the chat response's own `provider` field or other response metadata. It must instead refuse (exit with an error naming the call or pass and write no record), or leave that model's evidence absent and add a flag that names the pass. Mapping a provider that a non-null generation reply names to that provider's pinned host is NOT a violation.\"\n\n\"Judge only the code shown. Any path by which a missing/null generation reply still ends with an endpoint written from another source is a violation, even if guarded or 'confirmed' by other metadata.\"\n\nSo the key question: is there a path where a call's generation reply is missing/null/no-provider, and an endpoint is still written for that model from another source?\n\nConsider the checks path with a linked call whose generation reply is missing: `endpoint_of` refuses → `call.endpoint` is None → `ep_v = None` → no endpoint in check. Plus the refusal causes exit 3, nothing written. CONFORMS for that path.\n\nConsider `endpoint_of` for aggregator with generation naming provider → pin host. Allowed explicitly.\n\nConsider direct host: The endpoint for a direct call is written from the request URL host even though there's no generation reply. Is that a violation? The rule's framing: \"the `endpoint`: the host of the service that actually answered its calls\". For direct calls, request URL host = answering host. The generation reply is an OpenRouter artifact; the rule says \"Each logged call may carry a `generation` field: the raw reply of OpenRouter's /api/v1/generation endpoint\". For a direct call, no OpenRouter. I think the rule implicitly scopes to OpenRouter-routed calls — the whole point of the rule is that behind a router you need evidence of who served. A direct call's host is directly evidenced. Also, the rule's prohibited sources list doesn't include \"the request URL from the harness log\". And the docstring in the code explicitly says \"a direct call's URL host\" is the source. I'd judge direct host as conforming.\n\nNow the hand-entered endpoint paths (3 when call is None, and 5 readings checks). Do these constitute violations?\n\nPath 3-no-call: The pass facts hand-entered endpoint written when no call is linked. When no call is linked because the evaluator is a script — no model call exists, the rule doesn't apply. When no call is linked because link failed — refusal fires, nothing written. When no call is linked because the evaluator isn't in ev_calls (not a model or not listed)... \n\nSub-case: evaluator IS a model, listed, but its harness log exists with calls, yet the pass's `call` name isn't in the log → refusal → nothing written. OK.\n\nSub-case: evaluator IS a model, listed, but its harness log is missing entirely → refusal in evaluator_history → nothing written. OK.\n\nSub-case: evaluator listed as model but `e.get(\"kind\") != \"model\"`? Then ev_calls lacks it. If pass facts name it, link_call returns None, no refusal, hand-entered endpoint written. But the evaluator isn't a model... then does it have calls? The inputs say it's not a model kind (maybe script). Endpoint for a script's check — hand-entered. Rule doesn't apply (no model call). Hmm, but what if the inputs misclassify a model as script? We judge code, not data.\n\nSub-case: readings checks (path 5): hand-entered checks copied verbatim including endpoint. This is for \"a readings job\" — no §5 record. These checks are entirely hand-entered facts. If a model call's generation reply were missing and the orchestrator hand-entered an endpoint... the emitter writes it. Does the rule apply? The rule says \"the code must not write an endpoint for that call's model from anything else: ... not from hand-entered inputs\". The readings path writes endpoints from hand-entered inputs without consulting any generation reply. \n\nHmm, this is the crux. The question setup: \"Each record names, for every model that took part, the `endpoint`: the host of the service that actually answered its calls.\" The readings checks path writes hand-entered endpoints for checks — these are checks by evaluators, which may be models. There's no verification against the harness log or generation reply.\n\nBut wait — is this within the scope the rule cares about? The rule: \"Any path by which a missing/null generation reply still ends with an endpoint written from another source is a violation, even if guarded or 'confirmed' by other metadata.\"\n\nStrictly: if a model call's generation reply is missing (e.g., the harness log for the maker or an evaluator lacks generation), does any code path still write an endpoint for that model from another source? Let's check the maker/evaluator model blocks: no — endpoint stripped from inputs, and only log-derived endpoint (which requires generation for aggregator) is written. If generation missing → refusal → nothing written at all.\n\nThe checks path: for a pass by a model evaluator with a harness log whose calls have missing generation replies → `endpoint_of` refuses → exit 3 → nothing written. So no endpoint