ripts don't make model calls, the generation rule doesn't apply.\n   - unlisted evaluator: hard gap, exit 1, record written with hand-entered endpoint. If that evaluator was a model... the code has no idea. Hmm.\n\nFor (4): readings job checks — hand-entered endpoints verbatim. But as noted, for model evaluators the harness log IS read and aggregator-missing-generation → refusal → nothing written. For direct calls, endpoints written from hand-entered checks without cross-check — but direct calls don't need generation. Hmm, wait — but do readings jobs even have harness logs for evaluators? Line 619-621 adds a SOFT gap if no external harness log for a model evaluator. So a readings job CAN lack evaluator harness logs entirely (soft gap, exit 1... wait, soft gap means not hard → exit 0!). Let me check: `gaps.add(\"external\", f\"no harness log (CL1-15) for model evaluator {e['id']}\", False)` — hard=False → soft. Exit code: `if gaps.hard: return 1` — soft gaps don't trigger exit 1. So exit 0 with a note. So in a readings job, a model evaluator with NO harness log → soft gap, record written, and the hand-entered checks (with hand-entered endpoints) written verbatim.\n\nHmm wait, but for model evaluators, `evaluator_history` is called: log = find_log(... rel from e.get(\"harness_log\") or external entries). If none → `ref.add(\"time\", f\"{who}: a model evaluator needs its harness log ...\")` — that's a REFUSAL (ref, not gaps)! So a model evaluator without a harness log → refusal → exit 3, nothing written. So the soft gap at line 619-621 is unreachable for model evaluators (they either have logs — read with annotate — or refusal). Wait, unless `e.get(\"kind\") == \"model\"` but evaluator_history... no: line 387: `if e.get(\"kind\") == \"model\": eh = evaluator_history(...)`. And evaluator_history always either finds the log or refuses. So every model evaluator in ANY job must have a readable harness log with calls (or refusal). Hmm, and calls must have `at` or refusal per call. And `annotate` → endpoint_of per call: aggregator calls need pin + generation naming pin; direct calls fine.\n\nSo actually, for ANY model that took part (maker or listed model evaluator), the harness log is mandatory and aggregator calls are generation-gated. The refusal machinery means: any missing/null/no-provider generation on an aggregator call for a listed model → exit 3, nothing written. Strong conformance on those paths.\n\nThe leaks:\n- Pass-facts checks with unlinked call → hand-entered endpoint (only without refusal when evaluator unlisted or script).\n- Readings checks verbatim (hand-entered endpoint) — but model evaluators still need logs+generation; maker too. So in a readings job with model participants via OpenRouter, missing generation → refused. If all model calls are direct → no generation needed → hand-entered check endpoints written... but the rule only forbids writing endpoints from other sources WHEN generation is missing/null/no-provider. For direct calls, generation is N/A (not \"missing\" in the rule's sense — the rule says \"Each logged call may carry a `generation` field\" — it's OpenRouter's endpoint; a direct call to a provider has no generation reply, and the rule's trigger arguably doesn't fire for it since the endpoint source is the request itself... hmm, but literally \"when the generation reply of a call is missing or null\" — a direct call's generation reply IS missing (it's absent). Then \"the code must not write an endpoint for that call's model from anything else\" — but the request URL host is written. Is the request URL host \"anything else\"? \n\nHmm, this is the interpretive crux. Let me think hard about it.\n\nThe rule's purpose (from the question preamble): \"Each record names, for every model that took part, the `endpoint`: the host of the service that actually answered its calls.\" And: \"Each logged call may carry a `generation` field: the raw reply of OpenRouter's /api/v1/generation endpoint, which names the provider that served the call (provider_name, provider_responses).\"\n\nThe generation reply matters ONLY for OpenRouter calls — it's the evidence of WHICH provider behind the router answered. For a direct call, the host that answered is the request URL host — directly observed, no inference. The rule lists forbidden alternative sources: routing configuration, provider pin or its host, hand-entered inputs, chat response's provider field or other response metadata. The request URL host is none of these. And \"Mapping a provider that a non-null generation reply names to that provider's pinned host is NOT a violation\" — clarifies the aggregator path.\n\nMoreover, the code's own docstring: \"A call made straight to a provider: the request URL host.\" And custody SPEC 4 (quoted): \"endpoint is the host of the service that ANSWERED; 'a router that passed the request on is not the service that answered'\". So direct host = answered host. The rule would be nonsensical if it forbade writing the request URL host for direct calls — then NO endpoint could ever be written for direct-to-provider calls, which contradicts \"endpoint is the host of the service that actually answered\". So the rule's trigger must be scoped to calls where the generation reply is the relevant evidence — i.e., aggregator/OpenRouter calls. I'm confident: direct host is fine.\n\nOK so now the remaining suspects are the hand-entered endpoint writes in checks (unlinked) and readings verbatim checks.\n\nLet me reconsider the unlinked-call case (2). For the endpoint to be written with exit 0/1 (record written), we need no refusals. Cases:\n\n(a) Pass's evaluator is a listed script evaluator. Pass facts carry endpoint? Possibly hand-entered. Written. Script = no model calls → no generation → rule N/A. I think fine.\n\n(b) Pass's evaluator id not in the listed evaluators. Then: link_call None (no refusal), `ep_v = f.get(\"endpoint\")` written; plus gap \"not a listed evaluator\" (hard) → exit 1, record written.