ration True. names_provider True. other/tried checks. → pin host. ✓ allowed.\n\nSuppose generation MISSING, response provider = pin's provider. served = {\"response provider\": pin_provider} (non-empty). has_generation False → REFUSE. ✓.\n\nSuppose generation NULL (raw[\"generation\"] = None) → has_generation False → refuse. ✓.\n\nSuppose generation present but `provider_name` empty, `provider_responses` empty → gen_served empty → generation_names_provider False → refuse. ✓. Even if response provider names the pin → refuse. ✓ (explicitly: response metadata never stands in).\n\nGreat, `endpoint_of` is airtight.\n\nSo the only conceivable violations are the hand-entered endpoint paths that bypass `endpoint_of` entirely. Let me enumerate them precisely with line numbers:\n\nemit_custody.py:\n- Line 446: `at_v, ep_v, xc = f.get(\"at\"), f.get(\"endpoint\"), {}` — ep_v seeded from hand-entered pass fact.\n- Line 466-467: `if ep_v: c[\"endpoint\"] = ep_v` — written when no linked call overwrote it (call is None and no refusal — unlisted/script evaluator; or... wait, also when call is not None but call.endpoint is None? No: `ep_v = call.endpoint if call.endpoint and ... else None` — overwritten to None. So only call-is-None path).\n\nHmm wait, actually, let me re-check: when `call is not None`, ep_v is REASSIGNED (line 455). When `call is None` and `f.get(\"at\")` — the elif only sets xc, not ep_v. When `call is None` and no `at` — nothing. So ep_v stays `f.get(\"endpoint\")` whenever call is None. Written at 466-467.\n\n- Lines 512-528: readings checks verbatim — any \"endpoint\" key in the input check is copied.\n\nNow: for the readings path — a readings job with NO model participants? Then no logs needed at all, and hand-entered check endpoints written. But no model calls → rule N/A. With model participants → logs mandatory + gated. So the verbatim endpoints for MODEL checks... the check's evaluator could be a model with a confirmed log; the check's hand-entered endpoint is written without any linkage or generation check. Hmm! Wait: in a readings job, model evaluator E has a log with aggregator calls generation-confirmed to pin P (host H). The hand-entered check for E says endpoint: \"H2\" (some other host). The code writes \"H2\" verbatim. Generation replies are NON-null (they name P). The rule's trigger: \"when the generation reply of a call is missing or null, or names no provider\" — NOT triggered (replies present and name P). So writing \"H2\" from hand-entered inputs... the rule says when generation is missing, don't write from hand-entered. When generation is PRESENT, the rule says mapping the NAMED provider to the pinned host is OK — but writing a hand-entered endpoint when generation names a DIFFERENT provider? The rule doesn't explicitly govern that (it governs the missing/null/no-provider case). Hmm — \"Mapping a provider that a non-null generation reply names to that provider's pinned host is NOT a violation\" — implies the pin-host mapping is the allowed use. Writing a hand-entered endpoint contradicting the generation reply... the rule's negative scope is about missing/null/no-provider. The positive scope allows pin-host mapping. A hand-entered endpoint with generation present naming another provider — arguably outside this rule's scope (other rules might catch it, e.g., the cross-check refusal in the pass-facts path — but readings checks have no cross-check). I'll not treat that as a violation of THIS rule.\n\nOK so the crux remains: unlisted-evaluator / script hand-entered endpoints, and readings verbatim endpoints — do any of these constitute \"a missing/null generation reply still ends with an endpoint written from another source\"?\n\nLet me think about the readings case ONE more time, very concretely:\n\nReadings job. Maker: kind \"person\" (no log needed). Evaluators: one model E with harness log (all calls direct to api.openai.com — no generation replies exist; `endpoint_of` → URL host, no refusals). Checks: hand-entered list with a check for E carrying \"endpoint\": \"api.openai.com\" (or whatever). Code writes it verbatim. Generation reply for E's calls: missing (direct calls — no OpenRouter generation endpoint involved). Rule: \"When the generation reply of a call is missing or null... the code must not write an endpoint for that call's model from anything else...\" — the code writes the endpoint from hand-entered inputs! Ultra-literally: VIOLATION. Purposively: the generation reply is irrelevant for direct calls; the endpoint evidence is the request URL, which the code itself derives (the model block endpoint = URL host). The hand-entered check endpoint duplicates what the log shows. Hmm — but the code writes the HAND-ENTERED value without checking it equals the URL host. If the hand-entered value were \"api.openai.com\" but... whatever, direct calls have no generation concept.\n\nHonestly, I think the question-writer's rule is meant to be evaluated on the OpenRouter/generation-gating logic. The rule's core: missing/null/no-provider generation → no endpoint from pin/routing/hand-entered/response-metadata → refuse or flag. The code does exactly this via `endpoint_of` + Refusals + exit 3. The hand-entered endpoint in pass facts is used ONLY when no call is linked, which for model evaluators always co-occurs with a refusal; for non-model/unlisted evaluators the rule's subject (a model call) is absent.\n\nHmm, wait — but actually, let me reconsider the unlisted evaluator case once more. Is it really unreachable for a model? Pass facts `f.get(\"evaluator\")` — the evaluator that ran the pass. `ev_ids` = listed evaluators. If f's evaluator is unlisted → gap (hard). The pass facts also may carry `endpoint`. Written. Exit 1 — RECORD WRITTEN. So: a model (unlisted in the custody inputs' evaluator list) that took part — its check carries a hand-entered endpoint, record written. Its calls' generation replies — never examined (no log read for unlisted evaluators). So YES, this is a path where an endpoint for a model is written from hand-entered inputs with no generation evidence, and the record is written (exit 1, not a refusal).\n\nDoes the rule forbid this? \"the code must not write an `endpoint` for that call's model from anything else: ... not from hand-entered inputs ... It must instead refuse ..., or leave that model's evidence absent and add a flag that names the pass.\"\n\nIn this path: the model's evidence (endpoint) is NOT left absent — it's written from hand-entered inputs. There IS a flag (hard gap naming the pass's check and the evaluator). Hmm — \"add a flag that names the pass\" — the gap `checks[{pid}/{dim}].evaluator: '...' is not a listed evaluator` names the pass id. So it's \"flag + endpoint written from hand-entered inputs\". The rule allows flag only with \"that model's evidence absent\". Here the endpoint (evidence) is present (hand-entered). So violation?\n\nUgh. But hold on — is the \"call\" for an unlisted evaluator even established? The rule's trigger is \"when the generation reply of a call is missing or null\". For the unlisted evaluator, the code has NO call — it can't say the generation reply is missing. The endpoint written is from hand-entered inputs, full stop. Is writing a hand-entered endpoint for a check with NO call linkage a violation of a rule about missing generation replies? \n\nHmm, let me think about it from the rule's PURPOSE: prevent the record from naming an endpoint (who answered) without the generation-reply evidence, whenever the generation reply is absent. The unlisted path writes an endpoint with NO evidence at all. If the underlying call was via OpenRouter with a missing generation reply → exactly the prohibited outcome. The code has a path to that outcome. Under \"Any path by which a missing/null generation reply still ends with an endpoint written from another source is a violation\" — I'd say YES, violation.\n\nBUT — counterpoint: \"Judge only the code shown.\" In the code shown, for