ponse Date header nor a created time\") → refusal → exit 3. If read_log returns NO calls → refusal (\"records no call\") or unreadable → refusal. So empty named ⇒ refusals ⇒ exit 3. ✓.\n- name not in calls → refusal (\"no harness call of evaluator ... is linked to it\"). ✓.\n\nSo confirmed: for LISTED MODEL evaluators, `call is None` in the checks path ⇒ a refusal exists ⇒ exit 3 ⇒ NOTHING written. The hand-entered `ep_v` write at L466-467 is reachable without refusal ONLY for script evaluators or evaluators not listed in ci[\"evaluators\"].\n\n- Script evaluator: no model call exists for the pass. The rule governs \"that call's model\" — no call, no model. The endpoint hand-entered for a script's check — hmm, would custody inputs even put an endpoint on a script's pass facts? Unlikely, but the code would write it. Rule N/A (no model call). Not a violation.\n- Unlisted evaluator: the code flags a HARD gap naming the pass (\"X is not a listed evaluator\") — and still writes the hand-entered endpoint, record written (exit 1). The evaluator could be a model. Its calls' generation replies: unknown to the code (no log read). Rule trigger: \"generation reply of a call is missing or null\" — for the unlisted evaluator's calls, the code has no generation reply at all. Outcome: endpoint from hand-entered inputs. \n\nHmm, is \"no log read at all\" equivalent to \"generation reply missing\"? The rule's setup: \"The tool reads a harness log per model. Each logged call may carry a generation field... That reply can be missing or null.\" The rule contemplates the tool READING a log and finding the reply missing. For an unlisted evaluator, no log is read — the rule's machinery isn't engaged; instead the check is flagged as referencing an unknown evaluator. The endpoint written is the hand-entered one — which the rule forbids as a source \"when the generation reply ... is missing\". \n\nI think the STRICTEST reading of the judge instruction — \"Any path by which a missing/null gene