s say it's not a model kind (maybe script). Endpoint for a script's check — hand-entered. Rule doesn't apply (no model call). Hmm, but what if the inputs misclassify a model as script? We judge code, not data.\n\nSub-case: readings checks (path 5): hand-entered checks copied verbatim including endpoint. This is for \"a readings job\" — no §5 record. These checks are entirely hand-entered facts. If a model call's generation reply were missing and the orchestrator hand-entered an endpoint... the emitter writes it. Does the rule apply? The rule says \"the code must not write an endpoint for that call's model from anything else: ... not from hand-entered inputs\". The readings path writes endpoints from hand-entered inputs without consulting any generation reply. \n\nHmm, this is the crux. The question setup: \"Each record names, for every model that took part, the `endpoint`: the host of the service that actually answered its calls.\" The readings checks path writes hand-entered endpoints for checks — these are checks by evaluators, which may be models. There's no verification against the harness log or generation reply.\n\nBut wait — is this within the scope the rule cares about? The rule: \"Any path by which a missing/null generation reply still ends with an endpoint written from another source is a violation, even if guarded or 'confirmed' by other metadata.\"\n\nStrictly: if a model call's generation reply is missing (e.g., the harness log for the maker or an evaluator lacks generation), does any code path still write an endpoint for that model from another source? Let's check the maker/evaluator model blocks: no — endpoint stripped from inputs, and only log-derived endpoint (which requires generation for aggregator) is written. If generation missing → refusal → nothing written at all.\n\nThe checks path: for a pass by a model evaluator with a harness log whose calls have missing generation replies → `endpoint_of` refuses → exit 3 → nothing written. So no endpoint written.\n\nThe readings path: no harness log is consulted at all. There IS no \"generation reply of a call\" in scope — the code never reads a call. Is the condition of the rule triggered? The rule's condition is about a call's generation reply being missing. In the readings path, if the job's harness logs (external evidence) show calls with missing generation replies, and the hand-entered checks name endpoints... The emitter doesn't cross-reference. Hmm.\n\nThe readings path is basically \"trust the ledger entirely for these checks\". This predates/differs from the log-derived path. Does the rule as stated prohibit this? \"not from hand-entered inputs\" — the readings checks ARE hand-entered inputs. So writing an endpoint from them, when the generation reply is missing/null, would be a violation.\n\nBut hold on — in a readings job, is there a harness log at all? Line 619-621: `for e in evs: if e[\"kind\"] == \"model\" and not any(x.get(\"subject\") == e[\"id\"] for x in external): gaps.add(\"external\", f\"no harness log (CL1-15) for model evaluator {e['id']}\", False)` — a soft gap (hard=False) for readings jobs? Wait, this applies generally, not just readings. Hmm, actually the `external` list comes from `ci.get(\"external\")`. In a readings job, external entries name harness logs. The gap is soft (False) — interesting.\n\nAlso for readings jobs, `rec` is None, so `maker_history` is still called if `m.get(\"kind\") == \"model\"`! Line 342: `mk = maker_history(...) if m.get(\"kind\") == \"model\" else {}`. And maker_history requires the harness log; if missing → refusal. So even readings jobs need the maker's harness log if the maker is a model. And `annotate` is called → endpoint derived with generation requirement. So for the maker, the rule is enforced even in readings jobs.\n\nFor evaluators in a readings job: `evaluator_history` is called for model evaluators (line 387-391). Requires harness log. So endpoints for evaluator model blocks are log-derived with the generation requirement.\n\nBut the checks in a readings job come from `ci.get(\"checks\")` — hand-entered, copied verbatim with any endpoint. These checks are the evaluator's checks. The evaluator's model block endpoint is log-derived, but the check's endpoint is hand-entered. Hmm.\n\nWait, actually — would a hand-entered check even carry an \"endpoint\" key? The code copies whatever keys exist. The custody inputs' shape (docs/custody-emission.md) presumably allows endpoint in checks. The pass-facts path reads `f.get(\"endpoint\")` for passes, so endpoints in check facts are a real thing. For the readings `checks` list, the code doesn't strip endpoint. So yes, a hand-entered endpoint would flow through.\n\nNow, is this a \"violation\" per the rule as judged? The rule's trigger: \"When the generation reply of a call is missing or null, or names no provider\". In the readings path, for a model evaluator's call... if the harness log is in external evidence, the emitter does read it via `evaluator_history` for the model block, and `annotate` → `endpoint_of` would refuse if generation missing. So the whole record would be refused (exit 3) — nothing written. Wait, really? Let me check: in a readings job with a model evaluator and a harness log lacking generation replies: `evaluator_history` → `annotate(calls, who, ..., ref, pin)` → `endpoint_of` for each call. If a call is to an aggregator → refusals. If calls are direct → endpoint = request URL host, fine. So for aggregator calls with missing generation, refusal fires globally, nothing written. So the hand-entered readings checks with endpoints never get written in that scenario. Interesting!\n\nHmm wait, but only if the evaluator has a harness log that's found and read. `rel = e.get(\"harness_log\") or next((x.get(\"evidence_file\") for x in ci.get(\"external\") or [] if x.get(\"subject\") == e.get(\"id\")), None)`. If found → annotate. If not found → refusal. So either way, for model evaluators, either the log is re