ctually — one more path to check: the model BLOCK endpoints (`maker.model.endpoint`, `evaluators[i].model.endpoint`). These are the custody record's \"endpoint\" for each model (\"Each record names, for every model that took part, the `endpoint`\"). The rule: \"must not write an endpoint for that call's model\". The model block endpoint = `last.endpoint` / `first.endpoint` from the log, generation-gated for aggregators. If the model's calls are direct → URL host. If aggregator + missing generation → refusal. ✓.\n\nBut WAIT — subtle: `evaluator_history` returns `\"endpoint\": first.endpoint` — the FIRST call's endpoint. The model block endpoint comes from the first call. The check-level endpoints from the linked calls. Both gated. ✓.\n\nAnd `maker_history`: `\"endpoint\": last.endpoint` — last call. Gated. ✓.\n\nHmm, what about `H.endpoint_of` being called with `pin` for a seat, but the call is DIRECT: refuse (\"went to X, but pin says via aggregator\"). ✓ (consistent).\n\nWhat about a call to an aggregator with pin, generation present naming the pinned provider, but ALSO response `provider` field naming the pinned provider — fine, all served match pin → pin host. ✓ allowed mapping.\n\nWhat about generation present naming provider, `provider_responses` listing the same provider only → tried empty after filter → pin host. ✓.\n\nOK. Now let me also check: `pin_for` — the pin can come ENTIRELY from the live event log (`if not pin: pin = {k: live.get(k) for k in PIN_KEYS} | ...`). The pin's host is used as the endpoint only after generation confirms. ✓ allowed.\n\n`check_pin_order`: pin declared after first call → refusal. ✓.\n\n`routing_x`: records pin + requests under x-plumbline; bad routing = hard GAP (exit 1, record still written) — but the endpoint itself was generation-gated. The hard gap is about the request not asking for the pin — per the docstring, \"A routing request that did not ask OpenRouter for the pin alone with no fallback and ZDR is a hard gap (exit 1)