he pass fact references a call implicitly (the pass's evaluator call). The code fails to link it. If the evaluator is unlisted, the code never read the log — but the CALL still happened, and its generation reply is (from the code's perspective) unavailable/missing. The code writes the hand-entered endpoint. Hmm — but actually, wait: would the endpoint `f.get(\"endpoint\")` even be present in pass facts? The custody inputs' shape for passes: \"each filed pass: status, channel, provider, model, family, scores, void_cause\" (line 27-28) — provider, not endpoint. But line 446 reads `f.get(\"endpoint\")`, and line 452-454 cross-checks it against the call's endpoint — so pass facts CAN carry endpoint. It's hand-entered (\"hand-entered {f['endpoint']!r}\" in the refusal message). So yes, the inputs' pass facts may carry a hand-entered endpoint, and the code writes it when no call is linked.\n\nHmm, so scenario: pass facts for pass P, evaluator E (a model), call name MISSING from facts, E's log has 2+ calls, neither named \"eval_artifact\" → link_call refusal → exit 3. No write. Scenario: E unlisted → hard gap → exit 1, hand-entered endpoint written. Scenario: E is a listed SCRIPT → no refusal, hand-entered endpoint written (but script → no model call → the rule N/A... though, hmm, would a script's pass facts carry an endpoint? A script evaluator doesn't make model calls, so no endpoint needed. If hand-entered anyway, written — but the rule doesn't govern non-model endpoints).\n\nAnd readings checks: hand-entered endpoints written verbatim for a readings job — where model evaluators' logs are still mandatory and generation-gated.\n\nSo the question becomes: for a MODEL with a call whose generation reply is missing/null/no-provider, can an endpoint still be written? \n\n- If the model is the maker or a listed model evaluator: NO — refusal, nothing written. ✓.\n- If the model is an unlisted evaluator named in pass facts: the code writes the hand-entered endpoint (exit 1). But